      <div>
        <h1>PUBLIC HEALTH IN DATA</h1>
        <p class="papel">Analysis of 107 million hospital admissions funded by SUS, Brazil's free public health system, from 2015 to 2023: volume, cost and mortality by year, diagnosis and state.</p>
      </div>
      <dl class="ficha">
        <dt>Type</dt><dd>Data analysis</dd>
        <dt>Data</dt><dd>SIH/SUS, 2015–2023</dd>
        <dt>Tools</dt><dd>BigQuery, SQL, Python, Power BI</dd>
        <dt>Images</dt><dd>{{ prints | length }}</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <p class="aviso">Personal project with the open microdata of SUS's Hospital Information System (SIH/SUS), queried through Base dos Dados on Google BigQuery. Amounts in Brazilian reais of each year, not adjusted for inflation. The dashboard is in Portuguese.</p>

    <section class="secao">
      <h2>KEY FINDINGS</h2>
      <ul>
        <li class="linha"><strong>R$ 151.7 BN</strong><p>Spent on 107.4 million admissions between 2015 and 2023.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>COVID</strong><p>Only the 4th cause of admission by volume, but the most expensive of the period: R$ 10.7 billion. Its average cost, R$ 5,440, is almost ten times that of a normal delivery.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>PANDEMIC</strong><p>Admissions fell 13.5% in 2020 compared with 2019, and in-hospital mortality rose from 4.25% to 6.61% in 2021.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>SEPSIS</strong><p>The highest mortality among the most frequent diagnoses: 46.6% of admissions end in death.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>HEART ATTACK +71%</strong><p>Admissions for heart attack grew 71% from 2015 to 2023, while mortality fell from 11.7% to 8.2%. Births fell 27% (normal) and 24% (C-section).</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>STATES</strong><p>Paraná has the highest admission rate (73.9 per thousand people per year) and the highest average cost (R$ 1,763). Rio de Janeiro has the highest mortality (7.1%) and the longest stays (7.1 days).</p></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

